  "final_text": "I submitted **part-based** with 0.8 confidence. The answer came from a train-and-test transfer experiment run through `query.py`, which used 23 of the 60 paid queries. I didn't read the server's code.\n\nTasks that share parts also tend to give similar responses, so either learner type could explain transfer between them. To separate the two, I used the free `compare` command to choose training tasks:\n\n| Target | What I trained on | Response overlap with target | Examples needed before \u2192 after (mean) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1-S2-M3 | Tasks that together cover all 3 of its parts (L1-S1-M1, L2-S2-M2, L4-S4-M3) | Low (0, 0.06, 0.375) | ~50 \u2192 ~14 |\n| L3-S3-M4 (control) | Tasks sharing no parts with it (L4-S2-M2, L4-S4-M2, L4-S1-M2) | Higher (0.56 each) | 42 \u2192 ~37 |\n\nA holistic learner should benefit most from the high-overlap tasks. Instead the big gain came from shared parts, which is what a part-based learner predicts.\n\nLimits on the result:\n- **Noisy tests:** repeat tests on the same task after training ranged from 1 to 39 examples, so I averaged 6\u20137 samples per target.\n- **Thin control baseline:** I have only one pre-training sample for the control target. If the true value was nearer 50, the control would show some transfer without shared parts.\n- **No replication:** I only ran one part-sharing target. The other one I looked at, L2-S4-M1, also had high response overlap with the trained tasks, so it couldn't separate the two explanations.",
